Multi-Agent Memory platform workspace coordinator

Purpose:
- Give every cold-start agent one public-safe, evidence-bounded account of how RemoteEndpoints.com, LocalEndpoints.com, LocalEndpoints Connect, MemoryEndpoints.com, and MultiAgentMemory.com fit together.
- Prevent source-qualified work, stale packages, public contract declarations, and deployed behavior from being mistaken for one another.

Verification status: active workspace memory; the checkpoint below was freshly reconciled against local Git state, independent reports, and public production routes, but every release or deployment claim must be reverified before use.
Memory scope: cross-product intent, authoritative roots, trust boundaries, current source/package/installed/deployment/live gates, known divergence, and ordered continuation.
Public-safe status: safe for source control; contains public revisions and route status but no credential values, private identifiers, payloads, or deployment secrets.
Update route: replace stale checkpoint facts after a fresh multi-system audit; keep dated command evidence and long reports in the shared Improvement handoff or each repository's ignored report area.
Source of truth: direct human goal, current repository bytes and refs, live public version/release routes, governing `AGENTS.md` and `.uai` files, and independently sealed reports under the shared Improvement handoff.
Next action for agents: load the full `.uai` startup order, recheck Git and live revisions, then take the highest unblocked item in the ordered continuation below without reviving a stale package.
Must not expose: raw API keys, bearer tokens, pairing codes, FTP or signing credentials, private prompts, desktop pictures, tenant or device identifiers, private handoff payloads, or hidden deployment values.

## Product model and human intent

- RemoteEndpoints.com exists for unattended control of a computer running LocalEndpoints Connect while nobody is physically present at that computer.
- Installing the independent RemoteEndpoints plugin, explicitly enabling it, and completing one authenticated expiring pairing is intentional standing authorization. After that setup, normal prompts and actions must not wait for target-side human approval.
- RemoteEndpoints.com transports authenticated prompts, desktop pictures, progress, results, errors, receipts, and control messages. The installed Connect runtime runs the local AI and performs Windows work with the authority available to its signed-in Windows account; the website does not directly invoke Windows APIs.
- Install, enable, pair, Stop, Pause, Disable, rotate, revoke, and attempt recovery or rollback are distinct operations. Never imply that one performs another or that completed external effects are automatically reversible.
- The public Demo must be a session-local synthetic-data overload of the exact authenticated production UI and client contracts, not a parallel lookalike. Mock data must be unmistakable, no protected device API may be contacted, deterministic clean scene paths must remain directly testable, and optional translucent instruction overlays must not block controls or accessibility.
- Each capable Connect instance may run its own local `.uai`, hold its own MemoryEndpoints connection, and participate as an independent swarm agent. Do not reduce this to a single global process or writer.
- Active normalized agent names must be unique within their tenant/workspace scope. Each writable memory has an immutable identity and exactly one current writer lease. Separate true branches receive new immutable identities and retain parent identity, parent digest, and complete lineage, so distinct memories and legitimate branches may run concurrently.
- Agent-name ownership and memory-writer ownership are independent renewable leases with monotonic fencing. Every mutation must reject stale name and writer fences after expiry, takeover, reconnect, restart, delayed delivery, or credential rotation.
- This is pre-publication clean-slate dogfood. Use one best current contract, update every dogfood caller, remove superseded routes and compatibility paths, and do not introduce legacy support, low-quality patches, or deferred cleanup.

## Authority and checkout map

- `E:\Source\GH\Multi-Agent-Memory`: public GitHub edition for the free single-organization private-intranet MATM reference plus the MultiAgentMemory.com companion site under `sites/multiagentmemory.com/`.
- This public repository is not the authoritative private commercial MemoryEndpoints.com deployment source. Commercial customer, account, billing, hosted-operation, and private production implementation belong to a separately governed private source/deployment boundary.
- `E:\Source\Python\LocalEndpoint.com`: authoritative LocalEndpoints.com public-site source.
- `E:\Source\Python\LocalEndpointConnectScaffold-CSharp`: authoritative LocalEndpoints Connect desktop source.
- `E:\Source\Python\RemoteEndpoints.com`: authoritative RemoteEndpoints.com source.
- `E:\Source\Python\LocalEndpoint.com\agent-file-handoff\Improvement`: shared public-safe cross-system reports and handoffs. A report is evidence for its pinned bytes and named gate only; it is not automatic authority to package or deploy later bytes.
- This repository's default public-site and cross-system coordination identity is `MemoryEndpoints-Frontend-Agent`; backend/storage/deployment work uses `MemoryEndpoints-Backend-Agent`. The misspelled historical backend identity is read-only history and must not receive new work. Use either active identity only with its own configured lower-authority credential.
- Deployment credentials, signing keys, protected tenant credentials, and one-time pairing material are external authority and never workspace memory.

## Gate vocabulary

- SOURCE GO means the named source bytes passed the named source checks. It does not imply an immutable package, signature, clean installation, production deployment, or live behavior.
- PACKAGE GO means one immutable allowlisted package was independently matched to accepted source. It does not imply installation or deployment.
- INSTALLED GO requires exact installed-byte identity on a clean target plus the required real runtime checks.
- DEPLOYMENT GO requires a target-bound, single-use activation with a sanitized receipt and rollback/containment evidence appropriate to that product.
- LIVE GO requires current production revision/byte proof plus the named behavioral checks. A public OpenAPI or route declaration is not authenticated runtime proof.
- Never inherit a stronger gate from an older report, a different commit, a synthetic test fixture, a debug artifact, or another product.

